  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 310 AM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nStrong upper level ridging will remain over the central US through \nearly next week. This will keep conditions dry throughout much of \nnorth central Nebraska. While temperatures on Sunday and Monday will \nnot be as warm as the previous few days, 850 mb temperatures in the \n18 to 24 C range will continue to push into Nebraska. This will keep \nsurface highs above normals ranging from the low 70s in northwest \nNebraska to the mid 80s south of I-80 on Sunday. Temperatures drop \nslightly, but still above normal, into the mid 60s in northern \nNebraska to the low 80s south of I-80 on Monday. \n\nThe beginning of some cooler temperatures and increased potential \nfor precipitation will arrive by Monday afternoon as an upper level \ntrough pivots across the Great Plains. This will bring some \nscattered showers across northern Nebraska Monday evening and into \nMonday night. Drier conditions briefly return on Tuesday before a \ndeveloping low pressure system over eastern Colorado brings a better \npotential at significant rainfall Tuesday night through Thursday \nmorning. There is still uncertainty in timing and track of this \nsystem, but at this time the best potential for rainfall appears to \nbe during the day on Wednesday as the low deepens and pushes off the \nFront Ranges into Nebraska. Will continue to monitor this mid-week \nsystem for QPF amounts as well as potential flooding concerns from \nthis developing system. \n\nBeyond Monday, high temperatures return to near or slightly below \nnormals in the 60s to low 70s through next Thursday. Lows will \nremain slightly above normals (upper 30s) in the 40s through the \nmiddle of next week. At this time, not anticipating any frost or \nfreeze potential, but will continue to monitor temperature trends \nover the next few days and make any needed adjustments to the \nforecast if necessary.",
